Streamify - A Vanilla JS Twitch CloneStreamify is a lightweight, high-performance, front-end-only "Twitch-style" clone built with vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. It's designed as a technical demonstration of how to build a modern, responsive web application without relying on heavy frameworks like React, Vue, or Angular.The project runs entirely in the browser using mock data, with an optional minimal Node.js server to enable real-time chat via WebSockets.FeaturesResponsive Design: Mobile-first layout that adapts to all screen sizes (phone, tablet, desktop).Dark/Light Theme: Supports system preference (prefers-color-scheme) and allows manual toggling, with the choice saved in localStorage.Stream Discovery: Browse live streams on the homepage, filtered by category.Client-Side Search: A debounced search input to instantly filter streams by title, channel name, or category.Channel Viewing Page: A dedicated page to "watch" a stream, with a video player and chat panel.HLS Video Playback: Uses the standard <video> element with hls.js as a fallback for browsers that don't support HLS natively.Follow System: "Follow" and "unfollow" channels, with the state persisted in the browser's localStorage.Live Chat:Mock Mode (Default): Simulates a lively chat environment with periodic messages from mock users.Realtime Mode (Optional): Connects to a simple WebSocket server for a shared real-time chat experience.Accessibility: Built with semantic HTML, ARIA attributes, keyboard navigation, and focus management in mind.Performance Focused: Utilizes lazy loading for images, deferred script loading, and efficient DOM rendering techniques.How to RunThis project is designed to be as simple as possible to run locally.Mode 1: Mock Mode (No Server Needed)This is the easiest way to see the front-end in action. The chat will be simulated.Clone the repository:git clone [https://github.com/your-username/streamify.git](https://github.com/your-username/streamify.git)
cd streamify
Open index.html in your browser:You can simply open the index.html file directly from your file explorer.Note on HLS and CORS: Some browsers may block HLS playback from the local filesystem (file://) due to CORS security policies. If the video player shows an error, you can use a simple local server:# If you have Node.js installed, you can use this command
npx serve
Then, navigate to http://localhost:3000 (or the URL provided by the command) in your browser.Mode 2: Realtime Chat ModeThis mode enables the live chat feature by running a small Node.js WebSocket server.Prerequisites: You must have Node.js installed.Install dependencies:Open a terminal in the project directory and install the ws library:npm install ws
Start the WebSocket server:node server.js
You should see the message WebSocket server started on port 8080.Update the chat mode:In channel.js, change the chat mode from 'mock' to 'websocket':// In channel.js, around line 130
state.chatClient = new Chat({
    mode: 'websocket', // Change this line
    // ...
});
Run the front-end:Open index.html in your browser (using a local server like npx serve is recommended). Now, when you navigate to a channel page, the chat will be connected to your local server. Open the same channel in multiple browser tabs to see the real-time communication.Project Structure/
