Architectural DecisionsVanilla First: The decision to avoid frameworks was deliberate to demonstrate proficiency with core web technologies. It results in a very small bundle size and fast load times.Modular JavaScript: Code is organized into modules (/modules) to promote separation of concerns. For example, DOM manipulation is separate from API logic and state management.State Management: Global state is managed in simple JavaScript objects at the top of app.js and channel.js. For a larger application, a more robust state management solution (like a simple Pub/Sub model or a lightweight library) would be considered.CSS with Variables: A comprehensive set of CSS variables (design tokens) makes theming and maintaining a consistent design system easy. The styling is a hybrid of BEM-style components and utility classes.Data-Driven UI: The UI is rendered dynamically from the data.json file. This simulates fetching data from a real API and makes it easy to add or change content without touching the HTML.Limitations & Future ImprovementsNo Backend: The application lacks a real backend, meaning there is no user authentication, data persistence, or dynamic content. All data is mock.Chat Persistence: Chat messages are not saved. In a real application, they would be stored in a database.Video Source: The player uses a static MP4/HLS link. A real platform would have a complex video ingestion and delivery pipeline.Search: Search is client-side and only covers the data present in data.json. A real implementation would use a dedicated search service like Elasticsearch or Algolia.Scalability: While performant for this scope, a client-side application with thousands of streams would require more advanced techniques like virtual scrolling for the main grid.
